      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Georg Thieme Verlag KG Stuttgart, New York.",
        "AbstractText": "Chronic opioid use has been known to cause disturbed sleep as well as excessive daytime sleepiness. During induction and maintenance of opioid use there is a reduction of REM- and slow wave sleep. Central sleep apnea (CSA) has been reported in about 30 % of patients with chronic opioid use. Ataxic breathing and CSA are more prominent in NREM- than REM-sleep. CSA does not seem the sole cause of excessive daytime sleepiness in these patients. Further studies are necessary regarding the effects and consequences of chronic opioid use during sleep."
      },
